---
// About page. Oriented on the old site's three-part structure (it's about
// you / why miles / why us) but rewritten to the new site's rules: every
// number on this page is real by construction (program count comes from
// the collection), and there are no invented years, teams or trust stats.
// The client's published contact details (address, phone) stay OFF this page
// until they're confirmed on the client call — the request form is the
// contact path that always works.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const count = (await getCollection("airlines")).length;
---
<Base
  title="About us: buyflightmiles"
  description="An online miles agent with one job: getting people the airline miles they're short of, quickly, at a rate confirmed before they pay."
  slug="about"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:820px">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[
        { label: "Home", href: "/" },
        { label: "About us" },
      ]} />
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">About us</span>
        <h2>The miles agent that quotes first.</h2>
        <p>One job: getting people the airline miles they're short of, quickly, at a rate they saw before they paid.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>It starts with a shortfall</h3>
      <p>
        Most people who find us have a redemption in mind and a balance that doesn't reach it.
        The award seat is sitting there; the account is 30,000 miles short. Buying that shortfall
        at a fair per-mile rate is usually far cheaper than paying the cash fare, and helping
        people close that gap quickly and easily is the entire reason this business exists.
      </p>

      <h3>What are miles, anyway?</h3>
      <p>
        Airline miles, frequent flyer miles, travel points: different names for the same thing,
        the currency of airline loyalty programs. Normally you earn them by flying or through
        credit card spend, then redeem them for award tickets, upgrades, and companion fares.
        One-way awards are a quietly great use: most programs price them at half a round trip,
        while one-way cash fares are often punishingly expensive. Programs will also let you
        spend miles on hotels, car hire, and retail, but honestly, that's usually the worst value
        in the program; flights, and premium-cabin flights especially, are where miles earn
        their keep.
      </p>

      <h3>Why buy miles at all?</h3>
      <p>
        Premium-cabin award tickets often price a mile far below what the seat costs in cash.
        When a business-class seat needs 70,000 miles and the cash fare runs to several thousand
        dollars, miles bought at a fixed per-mile rate can cut the price dramatically. Not always,
        and we say so: every program page has a calculator that compares your real cash fare
        against buying the miles, and when cash wins, it tells you to pay cash.
      </p>

      <h3>How we work</h3>
      <p>
        We currently cover {count} loyalty programs across the major alliances. The process is
        deliberately simple, and deliberately conservative:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>You're quoted first.</b> Price your amount on the site, send the request, and we confirm your exact rate and delivery window before anything is paid.</li>
        <li><b>Nothing is charged on this site.</b> Payment happens after you approve the quote, never through the website.</li>
        <li><b>We never ask for your password.</b> Miles are delivered into the loyalty account you already hold; your membership number and account name are all we need.</li>
        <li><b>Program not listed?</b> <a href="/request">Ask us</a> and we'll check availability and price before you commit to anything.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The honest fine print</h3>
      <p>
        We are an independent miles agent, not affiliated with any airline or loyalty program.
        Rates shown with a tilde are indicative until confirmed in your quote. If you're weighing
        us up, start with <a href="/#trust">how every order is protected</a>, the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>,
        and the <a href="/blog">guides on the blog</a>, which are written to be just as useful if
        you never buy from us. Still unsure? <a href="/contact">Ask us directly</a> — we'd rather
        answer the question than have you guess.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wash-cool">
    <div class="container ctaband">
      <div>
        <h3>Ready when you are</h3>
        <p>Price your program, or ask about one we don't list yet. Quoted first, always.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#programs">See the programs</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
